WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.515
Why PDFs are hard.

2
00:00:01.795 --> 00:00:08.088
A PDF is a set of instructions for painting a page: put these glyphs at these coordinates in this font.

3
00:00:08.368 --> 00:00:11.184
It is not a document with paragraphs and headings.

4
00:00:11.464 --> 00:00:21.149
The pypdf documentation puts it plainly: PDF files have no semantic layer, so headings, paragraphs, and tables have to be inferred from positions.

5
00:00:21.429 --> 00:00:24.949
That one fact explains most of what goes wrong: Reading order.

6
00:00:25.229 --> 00:00:33.315
Two-column layouts, sidebars, and figure captions can come out interleaved, because the extractor has to guess which block follows which.

7
00:00:33.595 --> 00:00:34.512
Whitespace.

8
00:00:34.792 --> 00:00:44.776
Spaces are often positioning adjustments rather than characters, so words get glued together or split apart, and hyphenated line breaks survive as broken words.

9
00:00:45.056 --> 00:00:45.867
Tables.

10
00:00:46.147 --> 00:00:48.984
A table is usually just text placed at positions.

11
00:00:49.264 --> 00:00:58.309
A naive extractor flattens it into a stream of cells with no rows or columns, and the number “60” loses the label “days” two columns away.

12
00:00:58.589 --> 00:00:59.827
Headers and footers.

13
00:01:00.107 --> 00:01:07.211
“Confidential, page 3 of 40” repeats on every page and lands in the middle of sentences that cross page breaks.

14
00:01:07.491 --> 00:01:08.344
Scans.

15
00:01:08.624 --> 00:01:10.203
A scanned page is an image.

16
00:01:10.483 --> 00:01:17.629
A text-layer parser such as pypdf is not OCR software and returns little or nothing, without raising an error.

17
00:01:17.909 --> 00:01:30.048
pypdf is still the right first tool for digitally born PDFs: it is local, fast, free, and reads the real characters, which is more reliable than OCR when a text layer exists.

18
00:01:30.328 --> 00:01:34.979
The point is to know which of these failures your documents trigger, and to check for them.
